   "source": [
    "## Comparative Study of Deep Learning Architectures for Time Series Forecasting"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3937b746",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Introduction\n",
    "\n",
    "Deep neural networks are widely used for time series forecasting because they can learn temporal\n",
    "dependencies without hand-engineered features. But architecture choice is often justified by\n",
    "intuition rather than evidence, and naive comparisons between models can be misleading if the\n",
    "evaluation setup itself has flaws (leakage, a trivial target, no baseline, single-split variance).\n",
    "\n",
    "### Objective\n",
    "\n",
    "We compare five architectures — Feed-Forward, RNN, LSTM, CNN, and ResNet — on a single-stock\n",
    "forecasting task, evaluated against two naive baselines, using walk-forward validation and\n",
    "multiple random seeds. The goal isn't to crown a \"best\" model; it's to see whether any of these\n",
    "architectures earn their complexity on this task, and to be honest in the write-up if they don't.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a03ac4b3",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Methodology & Threats to Validity\n",
    "\n",
    "This section states up front what could make the results misleading, and how we mitigate it.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Non-stationary target.** Raw price levels are dominated by trend/drift, so a model can score\n",
    "  a low RMSE just by learning \"tomorrow ≈ today\" — that's not forecasting skill. We instead\n",
    "  forecast **log returns**, which are closer to stationary, and reconstruct price for visualization\n",
    "  only, not for scoring.\n",
    "- **No baseline = no way to judge skill.** We include two naive baselines: **Naive-Zero** (predict\n",
    "  zero return, the classic random-walk baseline) and **Naive-Persistence** (predict tomorrow's\n",
    "  return = today's realized return, a momentum baseline). A model that can't beat both isn't\n",
    "  demonstrating forecasting skill on this series.\n",
    "- **Single train/test split hides variance.** We use walk-forward (rolling-origin) validation with\n",
    "  multiple folds instead of one static split.\n",
    "- **Single training run hides seed sensitivity.** Each neural model is trained with 3 random seeds\n",
    "  per fold; we report mean ± std, not a single number.\n",
    "- **Fair comparison across architectures.** Every model — FFN, RNN, LSTM, CNN, ResNet — is given\n",
    "  the *same* lookback window and the *same* scaling/train/val/test split. In the original version\n",
    "  of this notebook, the FFN used a 100-step window while the RNN/LSTM/CNN/ResNet were accidentally\n",
    "  fit on 1-step inputs with labels equal to the inputs (a reconstruction task, not forecasting) —\n",
    "  that bug is fixed here.\n",
    "- **Data leakage in scaling.** Scalers are fit on the training fold only, per walk-forward split,\n",
    "  and applied to validation/test — never fit on the full series up front.\n",
    "- **Single scalar metric hides the story.** We report RMSE, MAE, and directional accuracy\n",
    "  (did the model get the sign of the move right?), since for a trading-relevant task, getting the\n",
    "  direction right often matters more than minimizing squared error on magnitude.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
