    "## Why We Forecast Returns, Not Price Levels\n",
    "\n",
    "Adjusted close price is highly non-stationary — it trends over decades, so a model (or even a\n",
    "constant \"predict yesterday's price\" rule) can look deceptively accurate on raw RMSE. We instead\n",
    "model **log returns**, `r_t = log(p_t) - log(p_{t-1})`, which are much closer to stationary and are\n",
    "the standard target in the forecasting literature for this reason.\n",
    "\n",
    "We confirm this with an Augmented Dickey-Fuller test on both series (lower p-value / more negative\n",
    "statistic = more evidence of stationarity).\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "fe4a5f6e",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "log_price = np.log(df_time_series[\"adjusted_close\"].values)\n",
    "log_returns = np.diff(log_price)  # length N-1, log_returns[i] corresponds to price[i+1]\n",
    "\n",
    "if HAVE_STATSMODELS:\n",
    "    adf_price = adfuller(log_price)\n",
    "    adf_returns = adfuller(log_returns)\n",
    "    print(f\"ADF p-value, log price:   {adf_price[1]:.4f}\")\n",
    "    print(f\"ADF p-value, log returns: {adf_returns[1]:.4f}\")\n",
    "else:\n",
    "    print(\"statsmodels not installed; skipping formal ADF test (log returns are used regardless, \"\n",
    "          \"as the standard practice for this reason).\")\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(16, 8), sharex=False)\n",
    "axes[0].plot(log_price)\n",
    "axes[0].set_title(\"Log price (visibly trending / non-stationary)\")\n",
    "axes[1].plot(log_returns)\n",
    "axes[1].set_title(\"Log returns (mean-reverting around ~0, closer to stationary)\")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
